{
 "name": "spin_quads_geometry_parity",
 "face_ring_start": "ignored",
 "face_ring_start_note": "A spin rewires two faces without moving a vertex, and the rewired rings are where this family's whole assertion lives. Measured 2026-08-18 they hold the same corners and the same winding as the reference's and begin at different ones in all eight cases (18 of 44 rings). The rewiring is pinned; the start of the rewired ring is an unread law -- doc/tasks/backlog/1311-ring-start-laws-to-capture.md.",
 "description": "Rotating a shared edge to the other diagonal of its two faces. Counts never move, so the frozen FACE RINGS are the whole assertion -- a position set would pass on a spin that did nothing. GEOMETRY CHANNEL ONLY.",
 "source": "frozen reference capture (task 1121, nine-tool sweep)",
 "provenance": {
  "schema": 1,
  "source": "live-capture",
  "reference": "ref-editor@11.2v3",
  "method": "command",
  "captured_utc": "2026-08-18",
  "harness": "tools/local/capture (tasks 1121/1123)",
  "task": "1160",
  "notes": "Includes the two spokes of a valence-6 and a valence-8 pole, two disjoint edges spun in one command, and the same edge spun twice with a re-selection between -- the cell that showed the operation is repeatable at all. CHANNELS: the sweep's comparison was never vertex-only -- it compared the vertex multiset, the edge multiset, faces BOTH as ordered rings (winding shows) and as unordered position sets, the material partition, the per-face 'did this face's own tag change', and the post-command selection. Re-checked independently over all 215 frozen cases: ZERO agree on vertices while differing on ordered face rings, so nothing frozen here is a vertex-channel-only agreement. PRECISION: the sweep compared at 1e-4 and this fixture's tolerance matches it. The actual reproduction is far tighter -- 208 of the 215 cases are bit-identical at the dumps' 6 decimals and the remaining 7 agree to 3e-6 or better, that being the worst deviation anywhere in the set. The tolerance is the MEASUREMENT's bound, not the data's."
 },
